1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,500
into sugar, fuel, plastic, and enough foodstuff to fill a supermarket.

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:10,480
It doesn't exist naturally. Corn is entirely a man-made phenomenon.

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:15,980
We grow it on the farm, in a lab, or even underground.

4
00:00:17,380 --> 00:00:22,100
On the cob, in a can, in a sack, or in a silo.

5
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:25,100
It's a worldwide gold rush.

6
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,980
Ethanol is about ready to take over as the number one user of corn.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,600
in the United States.

8
00:00:31,601 --> 00:00:34,240
Now, corn on Modern Marvelous.

9
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:50,920
Corn is the largest agricultural crop in the United States.

10
00:00:50,921 --> 00:00:54,960
Fields of corn cover more than 80 million acres of land.

11
00:00:54,961 --> 00:00:59,160
That's an area larger than the state of New Mexico.

12
00:00:59,161 --> 00:01:01,500
Pass that corn on the cob.

13
00:01:01,501 --> 00:01:03,600
It's the delicious treat we call sweet corn.

14
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:09,100
Lakeside Foods in Wisconsin has been processing sweet corn

15
00:01:09,101 --> 00:01:12,180
for over 100 years.

16
00:01:12,181 --> 00:01:16,020
Here, corn is king.

17
00:01:16,021 --> 00:01:19,320
This is where corn comes to get canned.

18
00:01:19,321 --> 00:01:21,980
And to make sure your sweet corn stays sweet,

19
00:01:21,981 --> 00:01:24,760
it's an all-out war against the clock,

20
00:01:24,761 --> 00:01:27,360
starting with the instant it's harvesting.

21
00:01:27,361 --> 00:01:30,420
The critical time for processing is within five or six hours.

22
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:32,940
We want to retain the sweetness of the corn.

23
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:37,360
We don't want the sugars to turn to starch by being delayed in harvest.

24
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:40,800
This is the first part of the process as we've had the corn wait.

25
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:42,480
We're loading it on the dock.

26
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:46,280
Now we're starting to push it into a conveyor, a vibrating conveyor.

27
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:51,380
This vibrating conveyor system causes the cobs to continually rotate,

28
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:55,220
so the weight of the cob doesn't damage the kernels resting on the belt.

29
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:03,540
Once inside the processing plant, the sweet ears of corn journey into a carnival of precision

30
00:02:03,541 --> 00:02:04,540
automation.

31
00:02:04,541 --> 00:02:08,540
As the corn comes into the corn room, we're going into the husker.

32
00:02:08,541 --> 00:02:10,540
The husker has six lanes.

33
00:02:10,541 --> 00:02:14,680
The lanes are separated by a series of neoprene and rubber rolls.

34
00:02:14,681 --> 00:02:19,880
What the rolls are doing in opposite directions are stripping the silk and the husk material

35
00:02:19,881 --> 00:02:21,360
off the ear of corn.

36
00:02:21,361 --> 00:02:24,400
Then it goes into a conveyor to further process.

37
00:02:24,401 --> 00:02:27,540
On average, in an hour, we're processing approximately 35

38
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:30,900
tons of corn out of the four Huskers.

39
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,640
Surprisingly, almost all of the delicate corn kernels

40
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:36,340
remain intact after this seemingly violent step.

41
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:41,380
Next stop, the cutter.

42
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:43,560
Ears must be positioned in the correct direction

43
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:47,760
to meet the fast and furious razor sharp blades.

44
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:49,760
Each cutter has a separate cutting head,

45
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:51,760
which includes six blades.

46
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:54,200
These blades are moved by a head and a gear

47
00:02:54,201 --> 00:02:58,200
which will open and close depending on the taper of the ear.

48
00:02:58,201 --> 00:03:01,200
Like magic, the ear goes in,

49
00:03:01,201 --> 00:03:04,200
and out come perfect corn kernels,

50
00:03:04,201 --> 00:03:08,200
still in a race to their ultimate destination.

51
00:03:08,201 --> 00:03:12,200
The cob will join the husks and silks on their own conveyor,

52
00:03:12,201 --> 00:03:15,200
bound to become part of a smorgasbord for livestock.

53
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:20,200
As we convey the kernels over to the next piece of equipment,

54
00:03:20,201 --> 00:03:23,200
it is a shaker which actually is separating kernels

55
00:03:23,201 --> 00:03:26,040
is removing pieces of top, tank, husk material

56
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:29,340
that may still be on the product screen.

57
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:32,240
Next, a series of wet washers and air cleaners

58
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:34,340
propel the corn into the inspection area.

59
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:40,420
Cameras reject bad kernels as they fly by at 600 feet per

60
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:45,960
minute, and human inspectors perform a final screening.

61
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:48,160
After the kernels leave inspection,

62
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:51,960
they're submerged in a flume of hot water that kills bacteria

63
00:03:51,961 --> 00:03:54,760
and raises the corn's temperature in anticipation

64
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:55,860
of the cooking process.

65
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,800
Now the sweet kernels are ready for the can.

66
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,200
The critical quality control program

67
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:07,700
is to maintain a certain head space in the can.

68
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:10,120
We need a temperature of a minimum of 80 degrees

69
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:11,820
before this goes to the cooker.

70
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:15,220
We also need a maximum fill weight of 10.7 ounces.

71
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:18,720
So those are critical factors in the operation of filling

72
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:21,960
and seaming this can.

73
00:04:21,961 --> 00:04:25,660
In the cooker, the kernels bounce inside each spiraling can

74
00:04:25,661 --> 00:04:29,060
in 225-degree heat for 16 minutes.

75
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:33,200
380 cans per minute enter the cooker.

76
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:36,160
The result?

77
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:38,740
Perfect corn in hermetically sealed cans

78
00:04:38,741 --> 00:04:41,180
with a shelf life of at least three years.

79
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:46,540
Total time elapsed from its arrival at Lakeside?

80
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:50,460
25 minutes.

81
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:58,960
This lakeside production plant will process approximately 40,000 tons of corn in 60 days or less.

82
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:05,580
Each year in the United States, almost 3 million tons of sweet corn are canned, frozen, and packaged.

83
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:08,640
That is a lot of corn.

84
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:11,200
But here's the surprise.

85
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:16,500
It represents less than 1% of all commercial corn grown in the United States.

86
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,940
So what's growing in all those cornfields?

87
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:21,880
It's called dent corn.

88
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:26,440
so named for a physical dimple on the end of the kernel.

89
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:31,220
Sweet corn is best when it's full of moisture

90
00:05:31,221 --> 00:05:32,800
and eaten right off the cob.

91
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:37,380
But dent, commonly known as field corn,

92
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:39,360
is just the opposite.

93
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:47,020
The U.S. grows almost 364 million tons of field corn per year.

94
00:05:47,740 --> 00:05:50,860
That's about 110 pounds for every person on the planet.

95
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:58,960
Field corn is an ingredient in over 3,000 foodstuffs on the grocery store shelf.

96
00:05:58,961 --> 00:06:06,960
You'll find it in everything from breakfast cereals to soda, to the batter on fish sticks,

97
00:06:06,961 --> 00:06:10,960
and even yogurt and ice cream.

98
00:06:10,961 --> 00:06:14,960
Every box of cornflakes contains 97% corn,

99
00:06:14,961 --> 00:06:19,960
and the cornmeal surrounding a corn dog is a fine grind of dent kernels.

100
00:06:19,961 --> 00:06:23,300
Corn starch sticks energy bars together,

101
00:06:23,301 --> 00:06:26,040
and corn oil will fry your chicken.

102
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:28,760
It's all field corn.

103
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:33,020
And more than 15% of America's field corn

104
00:06:33,021 --> 00:06:34,400
is shipped around the globe.

105
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:38,240
Corn is the most important crop in the world.

106
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:41,260
Farmers produce more corn than any other crop

107
00:06:41,261 --> 00:06:42,580
by a pretty long shot.

108
00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:44,040
Wheat comes in kind of second,

109
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:45,760
but kind of distant to corn.

110
00:06:45,761 --> 00:06:49,880
And the United States produces 40% of the world's corn.

111
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:57,180
In fact, a single U.S. corn farmer provides enough food and fiber for 143 people.

112
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:04,380
But as much corn as humans eat, it's animals that chow down most of it.

113
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:09,260
More than 70% of the annual corn crop will go to feed livestock.

114
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:14,780
But field corn and sweet corn aren't the only kernels on the cob.

115
00:07:14,781 --> 00:07:20,780
Perhaps the corn with the most fun appeal is a little delicacy called popcorn.

116
00:07:20,781 --> 00:07:26,780
We believe that the oldest kind of corn is popcorn, and that has a hard starch.

117
00:07:26,781 --> 00:07:39,324
It also has to have an intact covering around the outside of it in order for it to actually be able to build up steam inside the corn and have the corn pop to make a

118
00:07:39,325 --> 00:07:40,025
flake.

119
00:07:40,026 --> 00:07:46,780
Popcorn stores water in tiny pockets of soft starch.

120
00:07:46,781 --> 00:07:49,780
As the kernel is heated, the water turns to steam,

121
00:07:49,781 --> 00:07:53,780
and the hard outer surface withstands the pressure as long as possible.

122
00:07:53,781 --> 00:08:00,780
Then, popcos the corn.

123
00:08:00,781 --> 00:08:03,780
Popcorn comprises less than 2% of the United States corn crop

124
00:08:03,781 --> 00:08:06,780
and is grown in 25 states.

125
00:08:06,781 --> 00:08:08,082
But almost all U.S.

126
00:08:08,083 --> 00:08:13,220
corn is grown in the Corn Belt, which consists of 12 states in the Midwest.

127
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:22,200
Indiana, Illinois, Ohio, and Iowa produce 50% of America's total corn supply.

128
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:24,540
Iowa is number one.

129
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:30,540
It's very well adapted to the United States, especially the Corn Belt here in the Midwest.

130
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:34,500
It's really a tropical crop, warm season crop.

131
00:08:34,501 --> 00:08:37,400
But the growing season here, the precipitation,

132
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:40,940
the rainfall that we get, the amount of light that we get,

133
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:45,040
all those things combine to just be a wonderful, perfect

134
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:47,640
environment for it to grow in.

135
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:49,740
But no matter where corn grows,

136
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:53,520
adaptation and versatility have made it for some the 21st

137
00:08:53,620 --> 00:08:55,620
century's gold.

138
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:57,760
That's partly because it's not just for food.

139
00:09:00,260 --> 00:09:04,500
You'll find corn in over 400 non-food items, from adhesives,

140
00:09:04,501 --> 00:09:05,700
to explosives.

141
00:09:06,660 --> 00:09:09,000
There are a huge number of uses.

142
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:13,340
And what a lot of people don't understand is, in terms of starch,

143
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:18,300
there's more starch used in consumer goods, what we call bio-based products,

144
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:20,180
than there is as food.

145
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:26,360
Two-thirds of the starch, sold as starch, ends up in things like charcoal briquettes,

146
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:30,300
adhesives, paper, plastics.

147
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:31,580
Plastics?

148
00:09:31,581 --> 00:09:38,500
We think of plastic as a petroleum-based product, but corn will do the job too.

149
00:09:38,501 --> 00:09:46,500
NatureWorks in Blair, Nebraska, is a leader in the technology of transforming plant sugars into performance plastics.

150
00:09:46,501 --> 00:09:53,500
It's the first company to produce plastic from a 100% annually renewable resource.

151
00:09:53,501 --> 00:09:58,500
It's a new biopolymer called polylactic acid, or PLA,

152
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:01,500
that shares its name with a company that makes it,

153
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:02,500
Nature Works.

154
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:05,440
We produce a crop of corn in about 100 days,

155
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:07,440
not 100 million years.

156
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:10,020
The production process from planting a seed of corn

157
00:10:10,120 --> 00:10:13,460
to making PLA has a much smaller environmental footprint

158
00:10:13,560 --> 00:10:15,260
than oil-based plastics.

159
00:10:15,360 --> 00:10:19,420
We have reduced the consumption of fossil fuels by 65%,

160
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:22,500
and we produce 80% to 90% less greenhouse gases

161
00:10:22,501 --> 00:10:26,740
greenhouse gases in oil-based processes.

162
00:10:26,741 --> 00:10:34,880
Field corn is put through a milling process that separates the starch, oil, and protein.

163
00:10:34,881 --> 00:10:40,600
Enzymes added to the starch convert it to a type of sugar called dextrose.

164
00:10:40,601 --> 00:10:46,820
The dextrose is then fermented with yeast to produce lactic acid.

165
00:10:46,821 --> 00:10:52,060
A proprietary process removes excess water and crystallizes the lactic acid into the

166
00:10:52,061 --> 00:10:53,420
the NatureWorks biopolymer.

167
00:10:57,040 --> 00:10:59,600
Within a matter of days of a corn kernel being received

168
00:10:59,601 --> 00:11:02,340
on this site, then the corn milling operation

169
00:11:02,341 --> 00:11:05,980
is converted into the NatureWorks plastic.

170
00:11:05,981 --> 00:11:08,140
This bioplastic is shipped in resin form

171
00:11:08,141 --> 00:11:11,280
to customers around the globe.

172
00:11:11,281 --> 00:11:13,920
The resin is then converted into this family of products

173
00:11:13,921 --> 00:11:15,540
you see here on this table.

174
00:11:15,541 --> 00:11:20,180
Everything from plastic that can be used to make a gift card

175
00:11:20,181 --> 00:11:26,060
to a clear food container, to a foam-type product.

176
00:11:26,061 --> 00:11:28,160
We also have drink cups.

177
00:11:28,161 --> 00:11:32,000
The fiber made from PLA is real soft and easily printable.

178
00:11:32,001 --> 00:11:35,960
You see some shirts and other types of garments here behind me.

179
00:11:35,961 --> 00:11:40,700
It's easily identified by the NatureWorks logo.

180
00:11:40,701 --> 00:11:42,660
And this bioplastic costs about the same

181
00:11:42,661 --> 00:11:45,460
to produce as petroleum-based plastics,

182
00:11:45,461 --> 00:11:48,500
while decomposing in as little as 40 to 80 days

183
00:11:48,501 --> 00:11:49,700
when composted properly.

184
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:56,020
For companies like Wilkinson Industries,

185
00:11:56,021 --> 00:12:02,140
making plastic food containers starts when the nature works

186
00:12:02,141 --> 00:12:08,260
bioplastic is sent through an extruder, where it melts

187
00:12:08,261 --> 00:12:09,500
into a sheet of clear plastic.

188
00:12:09,501 --> 00:12:17,000
Cooled and measured for thickness, it's wound onto rolls and sent to a heating and shaping

189
00:12:17,001 --> 00:12:19,160
machine called the thermoformer.

190
00:12:19,161 --> 00:12:24,620
The last of the material exits the oven and goes into the mold assembly where the

191
00:12:24,621 --> 00:12:27,100
mold comes in contact with the material.

192
00:12:27,101 --> 00:12:31,500
It closes from the top and from the bottom.

193
00:12:31,501 --> 00:12:36,560
An air pressure and vacuum system force the plastic into the mold, and seconds later

194
00:12:36,561 --> 00:12:38,120
the plastic piece is complete.

195
00:12:38,121 --> 00:12:45,420
The finished containers are boxed and shipped, ready to appear on a deli or bakery shelf near you.

196
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:54,060
Corn may be the holy grail for plastic, but exploiting its full potential is still on the horizon.

197
00:12:55,840 --> 00:12:58,900
Under high heat, NatureWorks' performance is compromised.

198
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,920
So for the time being, you won't find it on a car bumper or on the space shuttle.

199
00:13:03,921 --> 00:13:09,260
What you're looking at here is really the first generation of NatureWorks polymers.

200
00:13:09,261 --> 00:13:14,200
We continue to do research and continue to expand the breadth of application.

201
00:13:14,201 --> 00:13:20,440
21st century corn is a marvel of science and agriculture.

202
00:13:20,441 --> 00:13:23,100
But it didn't start out that way.

203
00:13:23,101 --> 00:13:28,420
From the grasslands of Mexico to the mapping of its genome, corn has undergone an amazing

204
00:13:28,421 --> 00:13:29,420
transformation.

205
00:13:29,421 --> 00:13:34,520
And to create super corn is the next leap for both man and colonel.

206
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:43,780
The small baby corn sometimes seen in Asian cuisine and in salad bars is exactly what its name implies.

207
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:46,800
Baby corn is picked days after pollination.

208
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:52,220
If left on the stalk to grow, it would end up the same size as most sweet corn ears.

209
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:56,860
Corn will return on Modern Marvels here on the History Channel.

210
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:06,300
For 180 days, these wild-looking green stalks will flourish under the North American sky.

211
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:11,540
But the irony is that corn is anything but wild.

212
00:14:13,280 --> 00:14:17,820
Corn has been domesticated, which means it can't grow without human intervention.

213
00:14:18,820 --> 00:14:22,580
And not just some corn, but all of it, every kernel.

214
00:14:23,020 --> 00:14:27,320
There are no natural populations of corn like we normally see in an Iowa cornfield.

215
00:14:27,321 --> 00:14:28,720
It doesn't exist naturally.

216
00:14:28,721 --> 00:14:31,020
Corn is entirely a man-made phenomenon.

217
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:37,200
If we weren't here to plant, water, fertilize, and harvest it,

218
00:14:38,080 --> 00:14:40,620
corn would disappear from the planet within a few years.

219
00:14:42,220 --> 00:14:45,300
The hypothetical scenario would go something like this.

220
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:48,360
An ear of corn falls to the ground.

221
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:51,800
Each seed or kernel struggles to germinate.

222
00:14:52,700 --> 00:14:56,060
The tightly packed seeds rob each other of vital nutrients.

223
00:14:56,061 --> 00:14:59,760
Such brutal competition chokes most,

224
00:14:59,761 --> 00:15:01,600
if not all, successful reproduction.

225
00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:07,020
But corn will continue to thrive as long as farmers nurture it.

226
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:11,860
And companies like Pioneer Hybrid are on the job.

227
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:14,100
For almost a century,

228
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:16,980
Pioneer has used science to create better corn.

229
00:15:18,460 --> 00:15:21,100
As one of the largest seed producers in the world,

230
00:15:21,740 --> 00:15:24,660
they ship corn seed to every continent except Antarctica.

231
00:15:26,060 --> 00:15:34,380
This is a seed crop and not a grain crop, so we bring it in and carefully handle it to preserve the integrity of that crop

232
00:15:34,381 --> 00:15:41,120
so that when the farmer plants it the following spring, it meets all expectations that both we and our farmer customers have.

233
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:44,146
Corn is really an incredible crop.

234
00:15:44,147 --> 00:15:49,980
It's really dynamic in terms of what we can do with it to improve its characteristics.

235
00:15:49,981 --> 00:15:56,380
One way Pioneer works with corn is through plant breeding and the use of hybrid corn.

236
00:15:57,380 --> 00:16:03,740
When we talk about hybrid corn, which is really all the corn that we're using in modern society today,

237
00:16:03,820 --> 00:16:04,580
it's all hybrid.

238
00:16:04,780 --> 00:16:12,060
So it starts with two individual parents that are specifically mated to create great offspring,

239
00:16:12,420 --> 00:16:13,420
and those are hybrids.

240
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,080
Hybrids are the combination of two distinct strains of corn,

241
00:16:18,081 --> 00:16:20,280
each with its own unique characteristics.

242
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:24,600
One might have a very large ear,

243
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:27,500
and the other may have a very tall stalk.

244
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:31,660
Individually, these strains are known as inbreds.

245
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:35,880
Crossing these two inbreds together creates a hybrid.

246
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:39,740
And hybrids often exhibit heterosis,

247
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:42,160
which means they are better than their parents.

248
00:16:42,161 --> 00:16:51,200
this complex biological phenomenon of heterosis is the cornerstone of today's corn industry

249
00:16:51,201 --> 00:16:57,460
the wonderful thing for the seed companies is that if you collect the seed from a hybrid and

250
00:16:57,461 --> 00:17:03,180
plant it again it doesn't have the same degree of superior performance as the hybrid itself

251
00:17:03,181 --> 00:17:09,620
so growers have to go back to the seed company to buy hybrid seed again that's actually a good

252
00:17:09,621 --> 00:17:14,780
thing because seed companies have an incentive to invest in research and development.

253
00:17:14,781 --> 00:17:19,560
What modern day plant breeders did was they improved the plants ability to withstand water

254
00:17:19,561 --> 00:17:27,340
stress, nitrogen deficiency, nutrient deficiency, temperature stress, insect attack or disease

255
00:17:27,341 --> 00:17:29,600
attack.

256
00:17:29,601 --> 00:17:34,220
It may all look like modern science, but the breeding of corn really started thousands

257
00:17:34,221 --> 00:17:38,540
of years ago in central Mexico.

258
00:17:38,541 --> 00:17:41,960
Back then, corn looked a lot different.

259
00:17:41,961 --> 00:17:45,780
In fact, it didn't exist.

260
00:17:45,781 --> 00:17:50,020
It began as a wild grass called teosinte, which only has

261
00:17:50,021 --> 00:17:53,340
a few small kernels and no real cob.

262
00:17:53,341 --> 00:17:55,060
It's an extraordinary feat, the evolution,

263
00:17:55,061 --> 00:17:57,820
the domestication of a crop.

264
00:17:57,821 --> 00:18:01,640
I'm not sure if we decided to domesticate corn now,

265
00:18:01,641 --> 00:18:05,140
whether we could actually muster the will as a society

266
00:18:05,141 --> 00:18:06,500
to do this.

267
00:18:06,501 --> 00:18:11,300
It's one of the real gifts of our ancestors to us.

268
00:18:11,301 --> 00:18:14,380
In fact, scientists continue to study the Teosinti plant

269
00:18:14,381 --> 00:18:17,180
to unlock its evolutionary secrets.

270
00:18:17,181 --> 00:18:19,720
They have discovered that there's just a handful of genes

271
00:18:19,721 --> 00:18:22,580
responsible for the major morphological changes

272
00:18:22,581 --> 00:18:24,680
between Teosinti and corn.

273
00:18:24,681 --> 00:18:27,860
For example, the big one was the separation of the tassel

274
00:18:27,861 --> 00:18:30,060
from the ear.

275
00:18:30,061 --> 00:18:33,020
They probably saw mutations out in Teosinti fields

276
00:18:33,021 --> 00:18:35,060
that had the ear and tassel separate,

277
00:18:35,061 --> 00:18:36,200
and they selected for those.

278
00:18:36,201 --> 00:18:38,300
They may have noticed that they yielded more

279
00:18:38,301 --> 00:18:40,820
or had some other characteristic they were looking for.

280
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:44,380
The tassel, which produces the pollen,

281
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:48,380
is separated from the ear, which contains the egg cells

282
00:18:48,381 --> 00:18:50,640
that develop into kernels after pollination.

283
00:18:53,060 --> 00:18:56,200
The corn plant could and still can pollinate itself

284
00:18:56,201 --> 00:18:57,880
as well as surrounding corn plants.

285
00:19:00,980 --> 00:19:03,760
The Native Americans achieved the Herculean task

286
00:19:03,761 --> 00:19:05,460
of turning Teosinti into corn.

287
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:13,340
And they did it without a powerful and controversial modern scientific tool, genetic modification.

288
00:19:15,420 --> 00:19:20,420
If you talk about genetically modified corn, they would also be hybrids,

289
00:19:20,421 --> 00:19:25,260
but they're a special classification of a regulated product because we've brought in

290
00:19:25,261 --> 00:19:32,220
one single or maybe a few genes from another species to help us do something very specific in the plant.

291
00:19:32,221 --> 00:19:39,220
The revolution started in 1996 with what has become known as BT corn.

292
00:19:39,221 --> 00:19:47,220
The BT stands for Bacillus thuringiensis, a naturally occurring bacteria.

293
00:19:47,221 --> 00:19:55,220
This bacterium contains a protein toxic to one of corn's biggest enemies, the European corn borer.

294
00:19:55,221 --> 00:20:01,220
We express that protein in corn in a way that when the insect pest actually fed on the corn,

295
00:20:01,221 --> 00:20:05,720
it died as a result of taking that first bite.

296
00:20:05,820 --> 00:20:07,420
The corn borer has the ability

297
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:10,760
to destroy millions of acres of corn.

298
00:20:10,860 --> 00:20:14,060
But because of the Bt trait, the economic damage

299
00:20:14,160 --> 00:20:18,500
this pest can cause has nearly been eliminated.

300
00:20:18,600 --> 00:20:22,640
Genetic modification is an exacting laboratory science

301
00:20:22,740 --> 00:20:25,380
in which DNA from one plant or animal

302
00:20:25,480 --> 00:20:28,040
is transferred into the embryo of another.

303
00:20:31,220 --> 00:20:36,140
Pioneer Hybrid has over 300 corn hybrids available for farmers in North America.

304
00:20:37,680 --> 00:20:39,220
Many are insect resistant,

305
00:20:40,900 --> 00:20:44,720
defying bugs like the western bean cutworm that attacks the ear

306
00:20:44,721 --> 00:20:50,680
and the western corn rootworm beetle larvae that attack the roots.

307
00:20:52,820 --> 00:20:56,000
In Pioneer's so-called fast corn greenhouse,

308
00:20:56,660 --> 00:21:00,560
the company has accelerated their evaluation of genetically engineered corn.

309
00:21:01,220 --> 00:21:05,920
In one month, we'd actually test about 1,000 corn plants

310
00:21:05,921 --> 00:21:09,920
carrying about 100 different candidates to be traits.

311
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:13,140
When those get harvested, new ones show up the next week

312
00:21:13,141 --> 00:21:15,060
with new traits to be tested.

313
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:20,040
While future corn will certainly start in the lab,

314
00:21:20,680 --> 00:21:24,420
the genetically modified seed will have to be grown in test plots.

315
00:21:27,180 --> 00:21:30,660
Its cultivation near commercial cornfields is heavily restricted,

316
00:21:30,661 --> 00:21:33,120
since wind carries the corn's pollen.

317
00:21:34,680 --> 00:21:38,700
If untested, genetically altered crops could cross-pollinate

318
00:21:38,701 --> 00:21:40,460
with corn bound for the food supply.

319
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:43,460
The result could prove economically disastrous

320
00:21:43,461 --> 00:21:45,240
and potentially harmful.

321
00:21:47,080 --> 00:21:49,740
Scenarios might include massive food recalls,

322
00:21:50,240 --> 00:21:52,480
unknown medical reactions in the general public,

323
00:21:52,900 --> 00:21:54,360
and crop loss.

324
00:21:55,620 --> 00:21:58,480
In the case of plant-derived pharmaceuticals,

325
00:21:58,481 --> 00:22:00,780
Standard greenhouses aren't secure enough

326
00:22:00,880 --> 00:22:02,080
to grow certain seeds.

327
00:22:05,380 --> 00:22:08,720
So what about growing corn where the sun doesn't shine

328
00:22:08,820 --> 00:22:11,620
and the temperature always stays the same?

329
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:14,760
We are driving into the Marengo Warehouse and Distribution

330
00:22:14,860 --> 00:22:17,860
Center deep in the hills of southern Indiana.

331
00:22:17,960 --> 00:22:20,900
We're literally driving into the side of a cliff,

332
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:25,280
and up above us is about 160 feet of rock.

333
00:22:25,281 --> 00:22:28,940
This was once an active limestone mine

334
00:22:28,941 --> 00:22:32,980
that had subsequently been converted into a working warehouse.

335
00:22:33,740 --> 00:22:35,600
In the limestone caves,

336
00:22:35,601 --> 00:22:38,420
the temperature never fluctuates more than a few degrees.

337
00:22:39,260 --> 00:22:42,500
Sunlight never enters here, nor do any insects or animals.

338
00:22:44,140 --> 00:22:47,760
In this unlikely setting, founder Doug Ozenbaugh

339
00:22:47,761 --> 00:22:49,480
has created a contained atmosphere

340
00:22:49,481 --> 00:22:51,980
to grow any kind of corn for any purpose.

341
00:22:51,981 --> 00:22:56,360
It's called Controlled Farming Ventures,

342
00:22:56,820 --> 00:22:59,880
a blank slate to create any environment.

343
00:23:00,980 --> 00:23:04,360
And it all begins in the 2,000-square-foot test facility.

344
00:23:05,220 --> 00:23:07,260
For instance, if somebody was trying to develop

345
00:23:07,261 --> 00:23:09,800
a more drought-resistant line of corn

346
00:23:09,801 --> 00:23:12,940
and they came upon a valuable discovery

347
00:23:12,941 --> 00:23:14,640
on, say, January the 5th,

348
00:23:14,641 --> 00:23:17,200
with 12 inches of snow outside here in Indiana,

349
00:23:17,380 --> 00:23:20,880
we can give them a 95-degree day with 20% humidity,

350
00:23:20,881 --> 00:23:24,840
and we can do that every day for the next three months while they test their crop,

351
00:23:25,020 --> 00:23:28,940
and I can guarantee you that you're not going to get an emergency rain to screw up your experiment.

352
00:23:30,160 --> 00:23:34,160
Ozenbaugh's underground idea started in response to pharmaceutical research.

353
00:23:35,120 --> 00:23:35,820
The goal?

354
00:23:35,821 --> 00:23:43,100
Grow plants with special proteins for the safer, faster, and cheaper manufacturing of life-saving drugs.

355
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:50,500
We've been able to design a system and a facility for about the same price as a greenhouse,

356
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:55,720
that enables you to really control all of the important

357
00:23:55,820 --> 00:23:58,820
variables that affect plant growth.

358
00:23:58,920 --> 00:24:01,760
A growth chamber for the 21st century,

359
00:24:01,860 --> 00:24:05,500
controlled farming ventures puts corn into an alien world

360
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:06,500
and makes it thrive.

361
00:24:09,060 --> 00:24:12,400
But back above ground, what happens to all that corn

362
00:24:12,500 --> 00:24:14,200
that grows every summer?

363
00:24:14,300 --> 00:24:18,100
How does it get into our cereal, muffins, and donuts?

364
00:24:18,101 --> 00:24:28,500
From 1977 to 2007, corn crop yields grew by almost 60% from an average of 91 bushels an

365
00:24:28,501 --> 00:24:33,020
acre to 149 bushels per acre.

366
00:24:33,021 --> 00:24:40,900
Corn will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

367
00:24:40,901 --> 00:24:46,340
American farmers produce more than 364 million tons of corn each year.

368
00:24:46,341 --> 00:24:53,340
That's enough to carpet 130,000 square miles with kernels.

369
00:24:53,341 --> 00:24:59,340
We're farming here and been in the business 47 years.

370
00:24:59,341 --> 00:25:03,340
We start planting our corn in April.

371
00:25:03,341 --> 00:25:07,340
We always hope to be done by May 10th.

372
00:25:07,341 --> 00:25:11,340
That's a target date that we've set for ourselves,

373
00:25:11,341 --> 00:25:15,340
hoping that our yields will be better in the fall harvest.

374
00:25:15,341 --> 00:25:20,340
This year, the Winger's family farm in central Wisconsin

375
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:22,740
teams with 1,800 acres of corn.

376
00:25:24,840 --> 00:25:27,280
Not a kernel of it is the sweet corn

377
00:25:27,380 --> 00:25:30,180
destined to end up on your dinner table.

378
00:25:30,280 --> 00:25:33,300
It's dent corn, part of the 80 million acres

379
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:35,000
that will be processed into everything

380
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:37,900
from cereal to rat poison.

381
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:39,700
That is, if it doesn't explode.

382
00:25:42,700 --> 00:25:44,700
During the September harvest, they

383
00:25:44,701 --> 00:25:47,200
they store some right in their own backyard.

384
00:25:47,300 --> 00:25:49,140
In those three grain bands behind me,

385
00:25:49,240 --> 00:25:52,040
we can store around 250,000 bushel.

386
00:25:52,140 --> 00:25:54,940
There's not enough corn storage at a commercial elevator

387
00:25:55,040 --> 00:25:58,240
to store it all for a whole year's supply.

388
00:25:58,340 --> 00:26:00,440
So you need to store your own corn.

389
00:26:00,540 --> 00:26:03,160
And then at times of the year when the market is better,

390
00:26:03,260 --> 00:26:04,760
you can take advantage of that.

391
00:26:06,960 --> 00:26:08,860
When the wingers are ready to sell,

392
00:26:08,960 --> 00:26:12,860
one buyer is just down the road.

393
00:26:12,861 --> 00:26:17,860
Didion Milling has been grinding corn for 35 years.

394
00:26:18,860 --> 00:26:21,860
Each truckload Kelly Wingers delivers holds four acres worth

395
00:26:21,861 --> 00:26:22,860
of kernels.

396
00:26:25,860 --> 00:26:28,860
And its first destination is a giant dryer.

397
00:26:28,861 --> 00:26:31,860
That dryer has a series of burners and high pressure fans

398
00:26:31,861 --> 00:26:32,860
in the bottom.

399
00:26:32,861 --> 00:26:36,860
So the corn is actually in a layer between what we call skins,

400
00:26:36,861 --> 00:26:41,860
two skins, and the corn gets fed into the top and travels toward

401
00:26:41,861 --> 00:26:45,160
the bottom while hot air gets pushed up and out

402
00:26:45,260 --> 00:26:46,960
through the corn.

403
00:26:47,060 --> 00:26:49,300
We run the burners at about 180 degrees.

404
00:26:52,600 --> 00:26:54,540
Keeping the corn dry not only keeps it

405
00:26:54,640 --> 00:26:57,040
from spoiling too fast, but also from becoming

406
00:26:57,140 --> 00:26:58,880
a deadly hazard.

407
00:26:58,980 --> 00:27:01,980
If you have wet corn that's sitting in a silo very long,

408
00:27:02,080 --> 00:27:04,580
that's a perfect temperature to grow bacteria.

409
00:27:04,680 --> 00:27:08,620
So that bacteria, as it eats the corn, it creates heat.

410
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:10,660
And that heat will eventually get

411
00:27:10,661 --> 00:27:16,840
get to a critical mass where you can actually have a fire if you don't store the corn properly,

412
00:27:16,940 --> 00:27:18,224
if it's too wet.

413
00:27:18,225 --> 00:27:24,160
And with 21 silos that each hold the equivalent of 200 truckloads of corn,

414
00:27:24,760 --> 00:27:27,640
keeping this powder keg of kernels cool is a challenge.

415
00:27:31,480 --> 00:27:36,080
Disasters like this 1998 grain elevator explosion in Wichita, Kansas,

416
00:27:36,081 --> 00:27:40,960
that killed two people are a tragic example of the potential dangers.

417
00:27:42,520 --> 00:27:46,980
Corn can be kept for up to a year if the storage conditions are proper.

418
00:27:47,440 --> 00:27:50,160
So all these silos are equipped with aeration fans

419
00:27:50,161 --> 00:27:53,440
where we push cubic feet of air up through the corn,

420
00:27:53,820 --> 00:27:57,460
and that keeps the corn from having temperature spikes.

421
00:27:58,400 --> 00:28:01,800
These concrete monsters are as tall as a 12-story building.

422
00:28:01,801 --> 00:28:07,800
It takes two square miles of corn to fill just one silo.

423
00:28:07,801 --> 00:28:14,800
Corn is sent down this gravity chute into the mill system, and the first step that goes

424
00:28:14,801 --> 00:28:16,800
through there is the corn cleaning.

425
00:28:16,801 --> 00:28:23,920
Once cleaned, the kernels are cut into small pieces and sent through a complex system

426
00:28:23,921 --> 00:28:33,900
of sifters and air pressure chambers on their way to the grinding machines.

427
00:28:33,901 --> 00:28:39,520
In each grinding machine, two metal cylinders are positioned side by side and rotate toward

428
00:28:39,521 --> 00:28:44,000
each other at different speeds.

429
00:28:44,001 --> 00:28:48,160
Each cylinder has grooves, which will determine how fine the grind will be.

430
00:28:48,161 --> 00:28:53,480
The end products that come out of all this process would be corn flour, which is the

431
00:28:53,481 --> 00:28:55,980
finest material that we ship.

432
00:28:55,981 --> 00:28:59,720
And which might end up in the batter of your buffalo wings,

433
00:28:59,721 --> 00:29:02,820
or even in your donut.

434
00:29:02,821 --> 00:29:06,600
They also produce cornmeal that could cover your English muffin

435
00:29:06,601 --> 00:29:09,700
or fill your corn chip.

436
00:29:09,701 --> 00:29:14,700
An even coarser cornmeal you can find on the bottom of a pizza.

437
00:29:14,701 --> 00:29:17,000
And when that coarse cornmeal is cooked,

438
00:29:17,001 --> 00:29:19,780
it'll puff up and get processed into your favorite cereals.

439
00:29:19,781 --> 00:29:29,100
Even as this corn can be turned into food, its sticky characteristics make it a perfect

440
00:29:29,101 --> 00:29:40,280
binder to hold together such products as porcelain sinks and even rat poison pellets.

441
00:29:40,281 --> 00:29:45,340
Whether Didion's milled corn becomes a goodie you eat or a product you use, they ship it

442
00:29:45,341 --> 00:29:48,680
to clients around the world via truck and rail.

443
00:29:48,681 --> 00:29:51,680
We're actually inside a railroad car right now.

444
00:29:51,780 --> 00:29:53,680
So the bags that you see coming across

445
00:29:53,780 --> 00:29:56,480
are coming directly off the packaging lines.

446
00:29:56,580 --> 00:29:59,120
These bags each weigh about 55 pounds,

447
00:29:59,220 --> 00:30:02,020
and a bushel of corn weighs about 56 pounds.

448
00:30:02,120 --> 00:30:06,540
The railroad car will hold between 2,500 and 2,700

449
00:30:06,640 --> 00:30:09,940
of these bags, and that equates to an average tonnage

450
00:30:10,040 --> 00:30:14,340
of about 67 to 70 metric tons per railroad car.

451
00:30:14,440 --> 00:30:16,040
This loading is just the start

452
00:30:16,041 --> 00:30:20,040
of a global transportation phenomenon that literally

453
00:30:20,140 --> 00:30:23,380
helps feed the world.

454
00:30:23,480 --> 00:30:25,520
In collaboration with the United States Department

455
00:30:25,620 --> 00:30:28,860
of Agriculture, Didion Milling is one of the largest suppliers

456
00:30:28,960 --> 00:30:33,460
of US food aid to countries in need.

457
00:30:33,560 --> 00:30:36,800
They ship approximately 130,000 metric tons

458
00:30:36,900 --> 00:30:40,100
of a corn meal-based, nutrient-rich food product

459
00:30:40,200 --> 00:30:42,600
that provides essential calories to sustain life.

460
00:30:46,040 --> 00:30:52,020
The same Midwestern corn nourishing the masses now also feeds our gas tanks.

461
00:30:52,840 --> 00:30:58,940
The ethanol craze is here, but is there enough corn to fuel ourselves and our cars?

462
00:31:01,020 --> 00:31:05,880
The oldest corn cob pipe manufacturer in the world is in Washington, Missouri.

463
00:31:06,580 --> 00:31:14,040
It grows specially hybridized corn developed specifically for its large cob and produces over 5,000 pipes a day.

464
00:31:14,041 --> 00:31:17,880
Korn will return on Modern Marvels.

465
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:24,380
A near endless line of semi-trucks,

466
00:31:24,381 --> 00:31:28,380
filled to the brim with a bounty of gold kernels.

467
00:31:28,381 --> 00:31:32,200
This is the entrance to VeriSun Energy's ethanol production

468
00:31:32,201 --> 00:31:38,200
plant in Charles City, Iowa, opened in 2007.

469
00:31:38,201 --> 00:31:42,340
By the end of the decade, more than 200 ethanol biorefineries

470
00:31:42,341 --> 00:31:48,516
refineries will dot the landscape from Washington state to Georgia.

471
00:31:48,517 --> 00:31:50,820
Ethanol is a biofuel that

472
00:31:50,821 --> 00:31:55,183
reduces tailpipe carbon monoxide emissions by up to 30%.

473
00:31:55,184 --> 00:31:57,520
Ethanol is about ready to take

474
00:31:57,521 --> 00:32:03,240
over as the number one user of corn in the United States. Converting corn to ethanol

475
00:32:03,241 --> 00:32:10,540
isn't that complicated, but to mass produce hundreds of thousands of gallons per day requires

476
00:32:10,541 --> 00:32:14,940
is a detailed, coordinated, and streamlined system.

477
00:32:14,941 --> 00:32:16,920
Where we're at now is the starting point

478
00:32:16,921 --> 00:32:18,080
where everything begins.

479
00:32:18,081 --> 00:32:20,480
The corn comes in through here.

480
00:32:20,481 --> 00:32:23,660
A truck will weigh about 80,000 when it's full,

481
00:32:23,661 --> 00:32:26,080
which is about 30,000 for the truck,

482
00:32:26,081 --> 00:32:30,120
and about 50,000 pounds of corn.

483
00:32:30,121 --> 00:32:33,220
If you're doing the math, that adds up to 45 million pounds

484
00:32:33,221 --> 00:32:35,800
of corn a week.

485
00:32:35,801 --> 00:32:38,160
Speed through here is very important.

486
00:32:38,161 --> 00:32:40,540
Typically, when the time a truck pulls onto the scale,

487
00:32:40,940 --> 00:32:43,640
we sample it, weigh it, and a truck pulls off

488
00:32:43,641 --> 00:32:44,780
is less than a minute.

489
00:32:46,040 --> 00:32:47,600
Once the corn is okayed,

490
00:32:47,900 --> 00:32:49,880
the truck rolls over to the offloading area,

491
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:54,320
where it dumps its precious cargo into a pit.

492
00:32:56,380 --> 00:32:57,240
From the pit,

493
00:32:58,780 --> 00:33:00,820
the corn travels up a grain leg,

494
00:33:01,180 --> 00:33:03,180
where it flows into the top of the silos.

495
00:33:04,820 --> 00:33:07,040
We have four silos together.

496
00:33:07,041 --> 00:33:09,740
They hold about 2.2 million bushels of corn.

497
00:33:09,741 --> 00:33:16,360
We have a small fifth silo here where we actually screen the corn, get any impurities out, and

498
00:33:16,361 --> 00:33:20,280
we actually grind the corn into a corn flour.

499
00:33:20,281 --> 00:33:22,160
Behind me is our hammer mills.

500
00:33:22,161 --> 00:33:23,800
That's where we grind our corn.

501
00:33:23,801 --> 00:33:26,220
We have a dry mill process.

502
00:33:26,221 --> 00:33:33,720
Once the corn is ground into a flour, we send it over a conveyor where it enters our process.

503
00:33:33,721 --> 00:33:38,820
We grind about 120,000 bushels of corn a day.

504
00:33:38,821 --> 00:33:43,900
Conveyors move the corn flour into the cook area,

505
00:33:43,901 --> 00:33:46,600
where it's soaked with water and enzymes, which

506
00:33:46,601 --> 00:33:50,660
break down the corn starch into sugar.

507
00:33:50,661 --> 00:33:53,080
For hundreds of years, this basic process has also

508
00:33:53,081 --> 00:33:55,680
been used to ferment alcohol and make

509
00:33:55,681 --> 00:33:59,220
liquor, like corn whiskey.

510
00:33:59,221 --> 00:34:03,520
Now called mash, it cooks at 225 degrees Fahrenheit

511
00:34:03,521 --> 00:34:06,760
to kill any bacteria.

512
00:34:06,761 --> 00:34:10,460
The corn mash then cools and travels to an 800,000 gallon

513
00:34:10,461 --> 00:34:16,500
fermentation tank, one of seven where yeast is added.

514
00:34:16,501 --> 00:34:18,960
You can't make alcohol without yeast.

515
00:34:18,961 --> 00:34:21,640
The yeast eat the sugar, and one of their byproducts

516
00:34:21,641 --> 00:34:24,980
is the ethanol.

517
00:34:24,981 --> 00:34:28,940
After 48 hours of fermenting, it's termed beer,

518
00:34:28,941 --> 00:34:31,320
although you wouldn't want to drink it.

519
00:34:31,321 --> 00:34:34,720
After fermentation, one of the most important processes

520
00:34:34,820 --> 00:34:38,260
in ethanol production is actually purifying the ethanol.

521
00:34:38,360 --> 00:34:40,160
We do that behind me.

522
00:34:40,260 --> 00:34:43,160
We have three distillation columns.

523
00:34:43,260 --> 00:34:45,360
The first one is what we call a beer column.

524
00:34:45,460 --> 00:34:49,180
We actually separate the alcohol from the solids.

525
00:34:49,280 --> 00:34:52,380
The mash runs through a series of metal plates

526
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:57,220
and under high heat emits a water and alcohol vapor

527
00:34:57,221 --> 00:35:00,860
that rises and moves through two additional distillation columns

528
00:35:00,861 --> 00:35:03,940
to purify the alcohol to 190 proof.

529
00:35:07,220 --> 00:35:10,480
The final step sends the vapor to a molecular sieve.

530
00:35:11,420 --> 00:35:13,700
There, the water and alcohol vapors are separated

531
00:35:13,701 --> 00:35:15,820
as the water is captured and absorbed

532
00:35:15,821 --> 00:35:17,860
by millions of small clay pellets.

533
00:35:19,300 --> 00:35:22,640
What's left is 200 proof pure alcohol.

534
00:35:24,980 --> 00:35:26,500
Contrary to what you may think,

535
00:35:26,501 --> 00:35:33,140
ethanol isn't anything new ironically it was ethanol that fueled Henry Ford's

536
00:35:33,141 --> 00:35:40,080
early model T the first flexible fuel vehicle and Ford might be surprised that

537
00:35:40,081 --> 00:35:45,660
after a hundred years ethanol is in the alternative fuel category critics

538
00:35:45,661 --> 00:35:51,520
question is ethanol really environmentally friendly to grow the

539
00:35:51,521 --> 00:35:57,220
corn and convert it to ethanol requires nitrogen for fertilizer and diesel

540
00:35:57,221 --> 00:36:03,760
equipment on the farms as well as natural gas at the processing plant some

541
00:36:03,761 --> 00:36:08,500
experts estimate that it takes one gallon of fossil fuel to produce 1.3

542
00:36:08,501 --> 00:36:11,400
gallons of ethanol

543
00:36:12,160 --> 00:36:17,120
still there are already nearly 6 million flexible fuel vehicles traveling

544
00:36:17,121 --> 00:36:20,780
America's roads.

545
00:36:20,781 --> 00:36:23,280
They're called flexible because these cars are able to use

546
00:36:23,281 --> 00:36:27,880
either unleaded gasoline or the current ethanol standard, E85.

547
00:36:31,840 --> 00:36:35,720
E85 releases 80% less carcinogens, such as benzene,

548
00:36:35,721 --> 00:36:37,760
into the air than unleaded gasoline.

549
00:36:40,920 --> 00:36:45,980
In 2006, ethanol use in the US reduced CO2-equivalent greenhouse

550
00:36:45,981 --> 00:36:51,480
emissions by approximately 8 million tons.

551
00:36:51,481 --> 00:36:54,080
That's equal to removing the annual emissions of more than

552
00:36:54,081 --> 00:36:59,420
1.2 million cars.

553
00:36:59,421 --> 00:37:03,640
But is there enough corn to feed us and fuel our cars?

554
00:37:03,641 --> 00:37:05,340
We're not going to run out of corn.

555
00:37:05,341 --> 00:37:07,040
We have plenty of corn to go around.

556
00:37:07,041 --> 00:37:09,780
And if we start running out of corn, the price will go up and

557
00:37:09,781 --> 00:37:12,980
farmers will plant more acres to corn.

558
00:37:12,981 --> 00:37:15,280
Detractors, however, point out that this will mean less

559
00:37:15,281 --> 00:37:17,797
acreage available for other crops.

560
00:37:17,798 --> 00:37:22,240
Nevertheless, the corn ethanol story has an extremely useful

561
00:37:22,241 --> 00:37:23,141
co-product.

562
00:37:23,142 --> 00:37:29,120
Dried distillers grains are the portions of corn that can't be fermented,

563
00:37:30,240 --> 00:37:33,640
but they contain highly valued nutrients for livestock.

564
00:37:33,641 --> 00:37:35,680
This is where we actually process

565
00:37:35,681 --> 00:37:39,186
the distiller's grain and dry the distiller's grain out.

566
00:37:39,187 --> 00:37:41,440
The solids come out into a conveying

567
00:37:41,441 --> 00:37:45,120
system that takes it through two big rotary drum dryers

568
00:37:45,121 --> 00:37:47,140
will remove all the 10% of the water.

569
00:37:47,141 --> 00:37:51,560
So we have a 90% dry, solid product.

570
00:37:51,561 --> 00:37:54,440
You can see this, this is a mixture of protein, fiber,

571
00:37:54,441 --> 00:37:55,720
and oil.

572
00:37:55,721 --> 00:37:57,460
This is what the animals want.

573
00:37:57,461 --> 00:38:00,300
This has always been viewed probably prior to this industry

574
00:38:00,301 --> 00:38:01,500
as a waste product.

575
00:38:01,501 --> 00:38:03,260
But now it's just a viable product

576
00:38:03,261 --> 00:38:06,740
that we use in the feed industry.

577
00:38:06,741 --> 00:38:10,360
It takes a mere 65 hours to both process a kernel of corn

578
00:38:10,361 --> 00:38:15,760
into ethanol and ready it for shipping by rail.

579
00:38:15,761 --> 00:38:18,160
And it only took about 100 days for the corn to grow.

580
00:38:21,100 --> 00:38:24,040
Much of that corn is hybridized, specifically

581
00:38:24,041 --> 00:38:26,640
to get the ethanol to fossil fuel ratio higher.

582
00:38:29,120 --> 00:38:30,840
But in the future, it may be possible

583
00:38:30,841 --> 00:38:34,220
to convert even the corn stalks, leaves, and husks

584
00:38:34,221 --> 00:38:38,720
into ethanol fuel through the science of cellulosic technology.

585
00:38:40,360 --> 00:38:41,060
The U.S.

586
00:38:41,061 --> 00:38:46,920
government is investing hundreds of millions of dollars in research to try to figure out that question.

587
00:38:47,500 --> 00:38:54,780
Will cellulosic feedstocks play an important role in fueling our transportation, our cars and buses?

588
00:38:55,020 --> 00:38:55,980
We don't know yet.

589
00:38:57,340 --> 00:39:00,480
For now, kernels in our gas tanks fuel our cars.

590
00:39:01,260 --> 00:39:04,600
But corn converts to another fuel that may not be so good for us.

591
00:39:05,840 --> 00:39:09,520
In soda, candy, and about 2,000 other foods.

592
00:39:10,360 --> 00:39:15,440
It's sugar made from corn, a.k.a. high fructose corn syrup.

593
00:39:18,680 --> 00:39:23,640
Ethanol manufacturers are required to denature their pure alcohol before its shift.

594
00:39:24,620 --> 00:39:29,900
The denaturant can be anything that renders it unfit for human consumption, including gasoline.

595
00:39:31,500 --> 00:39:34,080
Corn will return on Modern Marvels.

596
00:39:34,081 --> 00:39:41,080
The soft drink industry generates almost $48 billion a year.

597
00:39:41,081 --> 00:39:45,080
Americans consume more than 15 billion gallons of its drinks.

598
00:39:45,081 --> 00:39:48,080
But what does soda have to do with corn?

599
00:39:48,081 --> 00:39:51,080
It's all about the sugar.

600
00:39:51,081 --> 00:39:55,880
On average, corn-based sweeteners comprise more than 50% of the U.S.

601
00:39:55,881 --> 00:39:57,080
sweetener market.

602
00:39:57,081 --> 00:40:03,080
And the most prevalent of all is that super sweet substance, high fructose corn syrup.

603
00:40:03,081 --> 00:40:08,360
Corn syrup can be found in an incredible array of your pantry's products.

604
00:40:08,840 --> 00:40:16,800
Everything from simple ketchup to mayonnaise to sports drinks to salad dressings.

605
00:40:17,360 --> 00:40:22,700
Not to mention ice cream, bread, and even baby formula.

606
00:40:24,100 --> 00:40:26,900
But there's a downside to the deliciousness.

607
00:40:27,460 --> 00:40:31,200
Some have targeted high-fructose corn syrup as a prime catalyst

608
00:40:31,201 --> 00:40:33,640
fueling America's growing problem with obesity.

609
00:40:35,940 --> 00:40:39,320
High-fructose corn syrup is manufactured from field corn

610
00:40:39,321 --> 00:40:42,960
through a process known as wet milling.

611
00:40:44,760 --> 00:40:48,380
The ultimate goal is to isolate the starch present in the kernels,

612
00:40:48,840 --> 00:40:51,060
since that's the stuff that will convert to sugar.

613
00:40:51,800 --> 00:40:56,240
The first step then in wet milling then is to take this corn

614
00:40:56,241 --> 00:40:59,580
and add it to an acidic solution of water.

615
00:40:59,581 --> 00:41:03,880
And this softens the grain so that when we go to mill it,

616
00:41:04,560 --> 00:41:09,540
what we want to do is keep that germ in as large a particle as possible.

617
00:41:11,300 --> 00:41:15,900
Like the yolk of an egg, the germ is the embryo and contains oil,

618
00:41:16,180 --> 00:41:18,720
while the starch resides elsewhere in the kernel.

619
00:41:20,660 --> 00:41:25,580
Here a specially equipped blender brinds the corn in water solution without damaging the germ.

620
00:41:25,581 --> 00:41:34,300
Once blended, the germ will float to the surface because of its high oil content.

621
00:41:34,301 --> 00:41:40,000
The isolated germ can then be squeezed to a pulp, and its corn oil will eventually wind

622
00:41:40,001 --> 00:41:42,060
up in your frying pan.

623
00:41:42,061 --> 00:41:49,060
The remaining portions of the kernel get finely ground.

624
00:41:49,061 --> 00:41:53,420
After filtering out the fiber, the liquid and starch separate in a settling trough.

625
00:41:53,421 --> 00:42:03,440
The result, 99.7% pure cornstarch, which is then processed on the molecular level into

626
00:42:03,441 --> 00:42:08,040
both fructose and glucose sugars.

627
00:42:08,041 --> 00:42:14,480
The combination of these sugars results in both corn syrup and high fructose corn syrup.

628
00:42:14,481 --> 00:42:19,600
The best way to think of it is a blend of different sugars combined with enzymes to

629
00:42:19,601 --> 00:42:20,600
make a liquid.

630
00:42:20,601 --> 00:42:23,180
It's plenty sweet.

631
00:42:23,181 --> 00:42:27,020
For example, you could eat this entire plate of apples.

632
00:42:27,021 --> 00:42:31,680
Six of these apples would equal the sweetening power

633
00:42:31,681 --> 00:42:34,360
of this little bit of high fructose corn syrup

634
00:42:34,361 --> 00:42:40,460
that is so nutritionally bereft of value.

635
00:42:40,461 --> 00:42:42,560
High fructose corn syrup has made its way

636
00:42:42,561 --> 00:42:45,440
into thousands of packaged food items.

637
00:42:45,441 --> 00:42:49,880
And some experts believe its chemical structure

638
00:42:49,881 --> 00:42:52,780
may trick the body in harmful ways.

639
00:42:52,781 --> 00:42:56,080
The theory is that high fructose corn syrup

640
00:42:56,081 --> 00:42:58,940
depresses the production of hormones

641
00:42:58,941 --> 00:43:00,440
that regulate our appetite.

642
00:43:00,441 --> 00:43:05,520
So we are actually less responsive biologically

643
00:43:05,521 --> 00:43:08,500
to the chemical messengers that tell our body

644
00:43:08,501 --> 00:43:09,900
to stop eating.

645
00:43:10,740 --> 00:43:13,460
And that's something regular table sugar doesn't do.

646
00:43:15,080 --> 00:43:16,760
But some argue there are other factors

647
00:43:16,761 --> 00:43:18,780
that contribute to America's weight,

648
00:43:18,781 --> 00:43:20,740
not just high fructose corn syrup.

649
00:43:20,741 --> 00:43:30,320
It deals with calorie intake, it deals with exercise, it deals with a whole gamut of nutrition.

650
00:43:30,321 --> 00:43:37,440
So people who are blaming one particular product I feel is erroneous and a wrong thing to do.

651
00:43:37,441 --> 00:43:43,700
The dilemma is that it's likely to remain a cheap and pervasive ingredient for years

652
00:43:43,701 --> 00:43:44,700
to come.

653
00:43:44,701 --> 00:43:49,340
Now the problem is that there are no commercially

654
00:43:49,341 --> 00:43:54,180
economical solutions to replace high fructose corn syrup.

655
00:43:55,380 --> 00:43:57,780
So with corn, it seems that every kernel

656
00:43:57,781 --> 00:44:02,080
is filled with promise, debate, and power.

657
00:44:03,160 --> 00:44:07,560
From super corn to biofuel, to feeding the masses,

658
00:44:08,600 --> 00:44:11,660
what we choose to do with this golden opportunity

659
00:44:11,661 --> 00:44:14,760
might just take us to a field of dreams.

